; THE X86 ELEMENT WIDTH (wintables-2026-08-25 §4a).
; expect: 0 2 5
;
; A boolean table is one byte per element on x86 and eight on the other three,
; because that host has no types and the width has to be carried. Losing it at a
; binder reads the array as qwords — seven bytes of the following elements per
; access — which is a wrong answer rather than a slow one.
;
; inputs: 0 3 9
;
; The buffer is created by `build`, threaded through a loop, and re-bound by a
; `let` before the counting loop reads it, which is exactly the chain that hid
; the bug: the counting loop's `c` is bound to a `build` TERM, not to a name.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build (@add n 1) (fn (c)
        (loop ((c c) (i 0))
          (@ge i n)  c
          else       (again (set c i true) (@add i 2)))))
    (fn (b)
      (loop ((acc 0) (k 0))
        (@ge k n)  acc
        (b k)      (again (@add acc 1) (@add k 1))
        else       (again acc (@add k 1)))))))
